WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:07.034
[MUSIC]

00:00:07.034 --> 00:00:16.307
[APPLAUSE]
&gt;&gt; Hello, hello.

00:00:16.307 --> 00:00:21.411
&gt;&gt; Hi, so I heard that the last time
that you were here on campus that

00:00:21.411 --> 00:00:27.125
you said that if you had, had
a Stanford MBA, you would rule the world.

00:00:27.125 --> 00:00:29.810
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH]

00:00:29.810 --> 00:00:34.889
&gt;&gt; I wish I could have gotten in.

00:00:34.889 --> 00:00:39.606
Yes, I think that to be in Stanford is

00:00:39.606 --> 00:00:44.182
such an incredible, lucky thing.

00:00:44.182 --> 00:00:48.256
And then I was told,
what did the dean just say?

00:00:48.256 --> 00:00:52.966
Who is it that just said that it was

00:00:52.966 --> 00:00:58.030
very important to also be rounded?

00:00:58.030 --> 00:01:00.020
&gt;&gt; Well rounded, right.

00:01:00.020 --> 00:01:02.557
Well, you don't have a Stanford MBA.

00:01:02.557 --> 00:01:03.374
&gt;&gt; No.

00:01:03.374 --> 00:01:06.415
&gt;&gt; And you basically rule the world.

00:01:06.415 --> 00:01:08.934
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH]
&gt;&gt; Do you think I should drop out?

00:01:08.934 --> 00:01:12.702
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH]
&gt;&gt; Well-

00:01:12.702 --> 00:01:13.578
&gt;&gt; It doesn't look like she's

00:01:13.578 --> 00:01:14.163
nervous, right?

00:01:14.163 --> 00:01:17.827
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH]
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH]

00:01:17.827 --> 00:01:21.145
[APPLAUSE]

00:01:21.145 --> 00:01:25.494
&gt;&gt; So DVF, you started your career very

00:01:25.494 --> 00:01:31.398
early on in working as
an apprentice in a factory.

00:01:31.398 --> 00:01:34.140
Can you tell us a little bit
about that experience and

00:01:34.140 --> 00:01:37.155
if you really wanted to be
a fashion designer growing up?

00:01:37.155 --> 00:01:38.275
&gt;&gt; I really didn't.

00:01:39.545 --> 00:01:45.192
When I talk to young people, I always say,
unless you have a real vocation,

00:01:45.192 --> 00:01:50.573
like you know you want to be an actress,
or you know you want to be a pianist,

00:01:50.573 --> 00:01:55.890
or something, or an engineer,
you don't know what you're going to do.

00:01:55.890 --> 00:01:59.915
And then when you start life,
there are all theses doors,

00:01:59.915 --> 00:02:04.696
and which door is going to be my door,
and which door are you going to push?

00:02:04.696 --> 00:02:09.617
And it's not always
the most glamorous door

00:02:09.617 --> 00:02:14.539
that will give you
the most glamorous life.

00:02:14.539 --> 00:02:20.593
I mean, when I met this Italian man and
he invited me to come and

00:02:20.593 --> 00:02:25.865
do an internship, well,
he had a little crush on me.

00:02:25.865 --> 00:02:31.423
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH]
&gt;&gt; And when he invited me to go over and

00:02:31.423 --> 00:02:34.860
to watch what he was doing.

00:02:34.860 --> 00:02:38.197
I mean, the last thing I
was going to think is that,

00:02:38.197 --> 00:02:43.180
that man and that factory would be
the most important thing in my life.

00:02:43.180 --> 00:02:48.080
So I think it's very
important to be very open and

00:02:48.080 --> 00:02:52.520
to be curious and to pay attention, and

00:02:52.520 --> 00:02:59.380
to kind of follow your heart and
follow what the universe tells you.

00:03:00.420 --> 00:03:03.910
All I knew is that I didn't
know what I wanted to do.

00:03:03.910 --> 00:03:06.570
I knew the kind of woman I wanted to be.

00:03:06.570 --> 00:03:12.100
I wanted to be a woman who could
have a man's life in a woman's body.

00:03:12.100 --> 00:03:14.630
I wanted to be able to pay my bills.

00:03:14.630 --> 00:03:20.023
I wanted to be able to do the same thing
as a man did, but I didn't know how.

00:03:20.023 --> 00:03:25.570
Then I found a way, and
I was very lucky because it happened for

00:03:25.570 --> 00:03:29.956
me very quickly as it does
now in today's world.

00:03:29.956 --> 00:03:35.699
I mean, so
many millenniums make it so early.

00:03:35.699 --> 00:03:39.189
And I mean at the time,
there weren't that many but

00:03:39.189 --> 00:03:41.390
I was one of them and I was lucky.

00:03:43.260 --> 00:03:48.426
So to that point, what the audience may
not know at that time was that you were

00:03:48.426 --> 00:03:53.708
also a princess and not a Sleeping Beauty
princess, but a literal princess.

00:03:53.708 --> 00:03:54.728
&gt;&gt; When I married a prince.

00:03:54.728 --> 00:03:55.735
&gt;&gt; You married a prince.

00:03:55.735 --> 00:03:59.092
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH]
&gt;&gt; It was a shortcut.

00:03:59.092 --> 00:04:05.650
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH]
[APPLAUSE]

00:04:05.650 --> 00:04:07.438
&gt;&gt; But you continued working, so

00:04:07.438 --> 00:04:08.268
why was that?

00:04:08.268 --> 00:04:10.045
&gt;&gt; Yes, and I married pregnant,
too, so that was-

00:04:10.045 --> 00:04:13.097
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH] I was going to leave out that

00:04:13.097 --> 00:04:13.805
part.

00:04:13.805 --> 00:04:14.747
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH] No, why?

00:04:14.747 --> 00:04:18.371
I mean, it's one of those things.

00:04:18.371 --> 00:04:20.978
I was engaged, okay,
so it's not something.

00:04:20.978 --> 00:04:22.673
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH]
&gt;&gt; But-

00:04:22.673 --> 00:04:24.029
&gt;&gt; Why was it so important for

00:04:24.029 --> 00:04:25.879
you to continue working, though,

00:04:25.879 --> 00:04:30.520
when you really financially-
&gt;&gt; No, no, no, I was just starting but

00:04:30.520 --> 00:04:35.234
I mean, I didn't want my financial
independence to come from my father,

00:04:35.234 --> 00:04:36.630
my husband.

00:04:36.630 --> 00:04:38.100
I wanted to do it on my own.

00:04:38.100 --> 00:04:39.560
And I did.

00:04:39.560 --> 00:04:45.630
It also made my marriage not survive.

00:04:46.870 --> 00:04:49.490
You have to say the things as they are.

00:04:49.490 --> 00:04:54.155
At some point I had to choose and
I made a choice.

00:04:54.155 --> 00:04:57.385
Buthe gave me two beautiful children, and

00:04:57.385 --> 00:05:00.890
we stayed friends forever,
and life goes on.

00:05:03.402 --> 00:05:08.227
&gt;&gt; I'm going to switch gears a little
bit and talk about your business.

00:05:08.227 --> 00:05:12.075
When you started your career,

00:05:12.075 --> 00:05:16.818
you created a very famous wrap dress.

00:05:16.818 --> 00:05:20.766
And here at the GSB we talk
a lot about product market fit,

00:05:20.766 --> 00:05:25.467
about creating products that
satisfy a particular consumer need.

00:05:25.467 --> 00:05:27.404
And looking at the audience,

00:05:27.404 --> 00:05:31.680
it looks like there's several
people with a wrap dress on now.

00:05:31.680 --> 00:05:34.394
If you have a DBF dress on,
can you stand up?

00:05:36.861 --> 00:05:43.662
&gt;&gt; [APPLAUSE]

00:05:43.662 --> 00:05:46.422
&gt;&gt; I think you found product market fit.

00:05:46.422 --> 00:05:51.050
[LAUGH] This dress has been relevant for
over 40 years.

00:05:51.050 --> 00:05:55.150
What do you think that you saw in
the market that other people were missing?

00:05:55.150 --> 00:05:58.385
&gt;&gt; My god.
It didn't happen like that at all.

00:05:58.385 --> 00:06:02.842
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH]
&gt;&gt; I mean, at all.

00:06:02.842 --> 00:06:09.130
I mean, okay, I was working with this man.

00:06:09.130 --> 00:06:14.160
This man had a print factory,
a printing plant.

00:06:14.160 --> 00:06:16.370
It was in Como, Italy.

00:06:16.370 --> 00:06:19.900
Como, Italy is basically the end
of the Silk Road, right?

00:06:19.900 --> 00:06:24.240
The Silk Road starts from China, you go
through all the thing, you go through, and

00:06:24.240 --> 00:06:26.050
then you end up in Como.

00:06:26.050 --> 00:06:30.140
In Como,
there were all the silk manufacturers.

00:06:30.140 --> 00:06:34.048
And therefore, around them,
there was an industry.

00:06:34.048 --> 00:06:37.082
It was all the illustrators and
painters and

00:06:37.082 --> 00:06:41.756
artists that would sell their drawings and
their paintings to those

00:06:41.756 --> 00:06:46.021
people in order to make beautiful prints,
scarves and so on.

00:06:46.021 --> 00:06:47.284
&gt;&gt; This is her print.

00:06:47.284 --> 00:06:48.219
&gt;&gt; This is a print.

00:06:48.219 --> 00:06:55.053
And so this man, so
that's what I learned first.

00:06:55.053 --> 00:06:58.817
He would go there and look and this,
and so they buy an artwork and

00:06:58.817 --> 00:07:02.261
then you put it in repeat and
then you talk to the colorist.

00:07:02.261 --> 00:07:07.723
And Italy at the end after
the war was very industrial,

00:07:07.723 --> 00:07:10.470
Italy was just beginning.

00:07:10.470 --> 00:07:14.487
And so there were these very, very small
factories that were being born and

00:07:14.487 --> 00:07:17.485
they were families, and
these people were colorists,

00:07:17.485 --> 00:07:21.088
and their grandfather was colorist,
and the great grandfather.

00:07:21.088 --> 00:07:25.130
So it was very like that,
and this one made handbags.

00:07:25.130 --> 00:07:28.700
So anyway this man had a printing factory.

00:07:28.700 --> 00:07:30.220
So I learned about that.

00:07:30.220 --> 00:07:32.860
Because it was a successful
printing factory,

00:07:32.860 --> 00:07:38.270
it would make all scarves for Gucci and
Ferragamo and people like that.

00:07:38.270 --> 00:07:39.960
He then needed more room.

00:07:39.960 --> 00:07:45.210
Because he needed more room, he bought
the factory next door, for the walls.

00:07:45.210 --> 00:07:50.560
When he went in, he saw all these
machines that weren't doing anything.

00:07:50.560 --> 00:07:55.044
Because the people who had the factory
before were making stockings.

00:07:55.044 --> 00:07:57.094
Now, you'll too young,
you don't know what this fashion is.

00:07:57.094 --> 00:07:58.374
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH]
&gt;&gt; But

00:07:58.374 --> 00:08:02.790
starting is what people wore
before they wear pantyhose, right?

00:08:02.790 --> 00:08:07.850
They were wearing stockings
with little garter band and

00:08:07.850 --> 00:08:13.480
so, stockings was over so
these people went out of business.

00:08:13.480 --> 00:08:16.330
And so
this man looked at these machines he said,

00:08:16.330 --> 00:08:18.830
what else could I do with these machines?

00:08:18.830 --> 00:08:22.230
Because they were tubular
knitting machines.

00:08:22.230 --> 00:08:27.290
And so he called in all the young people,
DuPont and people like that and

00:08:27.290 --> 00:08:30.830
they tried this machine
with a thicker yarn and

00:08:30.830 --> 00:08:34.730
that's how they came up
with the jersey fabric.

00:08:34.730 --> 00:08:38.080
And there are two ways,
there are two types of fabric,

00:08:38.080 --> 00:08:42.240
the fabrics that are woven and
the fabrics that are knitted.

00:08:42.240 --> 00:08:48.300
Jersey fabric is a knitted fabric,
it's what t-shirts are made of.

00:08:48.300 --> 00:08:53.770
At the time, t-shirts,
nobody would wear t-shirts, except

00:08:53.770 --> 00:09:00.171
mens as underwear, and maybe sailors,
but nobody else wore t-shirts.

00:09:00.171 --> 00:09:03.898
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH]
&gt;&gt; Nobody else wore a t-shirt.

00:09:03.898 --> 00:09:08.720
Until it was a very famous movie
star called Brigitte Bardot and

00:09:08.720 --> 00:09:11.730
she lived in this little
port called San Tropez.

00:09:11.730 --> 00:09:16.210
And there was a little shop there
when they started to dye the t-shirts

00:09:16.210 --> 00:09:17.820
in all colors.

00:09:17.820 --> 00:09:21.510
And there was Brigitte Bardot looking so
beautiful and so harsh,

00:09:21.510 --> 00:09:26.380
with these colored T-shirt that had
a little central peace signal on it and

00:09:26.380 --> 00:09:30.789
blue jeans and barefoot, and
that's when T-shirts started.

00:09:30.789 --> 00:09:36.100
So Mr. Faraday, my Italian man,
started to make T-shirts and

00:09:36.100 --> 00:09:40.210
then because he had a printing factory,
printed T-shirts.

00:09:40.210 --> 00:09:42.930
And I was there when all
of this was happening, and

00:09:42.930 --> 00:09:46.610
then he bought another factory
that used to make night gowns

00:09:46.610 --> 00:09:50.700
because they had very thin needles so
that he could wove this fabric.

00:09:50.700 --> 00:09:52.070
Okay, all that is,

00:09:52.070 --> 00:09:56.630
I didn't think any of this would
ever be important in all my life.

00:09:56.630 --> 00:09:59.854
That year my mother gave me a ticket,

00:09:59.854 --> 00:10:04.858
an airline ticket to go to
New York to visit my boyfriend.

00:10:04.858 --> 00:10:05.710
My boyfriend, the prince.

00:10:05.710 --> 00:10:10.295
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH]
&gt;&gt; So, I went to New York to see

00:10:10.295 --> 00:10:13.825
my boyfriend, the prince.

00:10:13.825 --> 00:10:16.447
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH]
&gt;&gt; Who was very good-looking,

00:10:16.447 --> 00:10:18.379
very handsome, he had everything.

00:10:18.379 --> 00:10:23.135
And you can imagine all the girls in
New York wanted to marry him., so

00:10:23.135 --> 00:10:25.625
they didn't like when I showed up.

00:10:25.625 --> 00:10:29.790
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH]
&gt;&gt; But I was invited everywhere and

00:10:29.790 --> 00:10:34.820
there were all these young designers
who wanted me to wear their clothes.

00:10:34.820 --> 00:10:38.400
And one was called Holsten and
Steven Burrow and

00:10:38.400 --> 00:10:42.120
all these generation Giorgio Sant' Angelo.

00:10:42.120 --> 00:10:46.180
This generation of young designers, and

00:10:46.180 --> 00:10:50.180
we didn't have clothes like
that in Europe and so on.

00:10:50.180 --> 00:10:55.920
So when I went back,
because after six weeks

00:10:55.920 --> 00:11:01.530
of visiting Egon, I went back to Italy,
went back to the factory.

00:11:01.530 --> 00:11:06.600
At that time I walked into the factory and
I just had a vision,

00:11:06.600 --> 00:11:10.340
I said, there's something I can do here.

00:11:10.340 --> 00:11:14.110
I can make samples and
I will try to go and

00:11:14.110 --> 00:11:17.260
sell them in America and
that's what happened.

00:11:18.390 --> 00:11:24.680
I started to stay late at night and work
with a sample maker, and the puzzle maker,

00:11:24.680 --> 00:11:31.130
grab any fabric that was left over on
the floor, and make a few samples.

00:11:31.130 --> 00:11:36.960
Then Egon came back on his way,
he was stopping for

00:11:36.960 --> 00:11:42.110
one bank, the next year I was going to go
to, let's ask, so we had to summer off, so

00:11:42.110 --> 00:11:46.960
he on the way to Asia, he stopped in Rome.

00:11:46.960 --> 00:11:55.160
We met in Rome, we got engaged and then he
went on, and I went back to the factory.

00:11:55.160 --> 00:11:59.780
And I'm going back to the factory, and
with this Italian man who's driving

00:11:59.780 --> 00:12:06.090
the yellow Lamborghini, and we go from
factory to factory, and this and that.

00:12:06.090 --> 00:12:10.607
And one day he drops me in Milan and
I'm feeling so dizzy,

00:12:10.607 --> 00:12:15.704
because I think it's a Lamborghini and
I fainted in the street.

00:12:15.704 --> 00:12:19.597
And I remember Italian people saying,
[FOREIGN], [FOREIGN] she's dead and

00:12:19.597 --> 00:12:20.135
I think.

00:12:20.135 --> 00:12:23.472
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH]
&gt;&gt; I was not dead, but it was pregnant.

00:12:23.472 --> 00:12:27.335
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH]
&gt;&gt; So, [LAUGH].

00:12:27.335 --> 00:12:33.466
&gt;&gt; [APPLAUSE]
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH]

00:12:33.466 --> 00:12:35.450
&gt;&gt; So, that's what happened.

00:12:35.450 --> 00:12:40.252
So then, all of a sudden,
my God, I can't believe it,

00:12:40.252 --> 00:12:44.671
I'm pregnant with this most
eligible man in Europe.

00:12:44.671 --> 00:12:47.738
Everybody's going to think I
did it on purpose and I can't,

00:12:47.738 --> 00:12:49.700
I can't, this is terrible.

00:12:49.700 --> 00:12:53.765
So I went to Geneva, I went to see my
mother, I said I can't I mean this is not.

00:12:53.765 --> 00:12:56.094
My mother said you're crazy anyway,
you're engaged,

00:12:56.094 --> 00:12:57.710
you have to let him know blah blah blah.

00:12:57.710 --> 00:13:01.340
Make the story short,
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH]

00:13:01.340 --> 00:13:03.920
&gt;&gt; The wrap dress.

00:13:03.920 --> 00:13:06.660
&gt;&gt; I said don't feel obliged, he said no,

00:13:06.660 --> 00:13:10.410
no no we're going to organize
the wedding for 15th of July.

00:13:11.710 --> 00:13:15.180
So at first I'm like how dare you,
but okay, fine, so we get married.

00:13:15.180 --> 00:13:18.560
So, I go back to the factory and
I said listen, I am pregnant,

00:13:18.560 --> 00:13:21.050
I'm getting married, and
I'm moving to New York.

00:13:21.050 --> 00:13:24.380
Will you please allow me to make
some samples from the factory?

00:13:25.520 --> 00:13:29.000
And that's what I did, and
that's how I made my first dresses.

00:13:29.000 --> 00:13:33.742
And they were basically
t-shirt dresses and

00:13:33.742 --> 00:13:39.115
shirts dresses and
I had no idea and no experience.

00:13:39.115 --> 00:13:41.735
Except I was 22 and I was cute so

00:13:41.735 --> 00:13:46.280
I knew what I wanted to wear and
that's what happened.

00:13:46.280 --> 00:13:48.541
&gt;&gt; But this happened in the 70s and

00:13:48.541 --> 00:13:52.433
this was the heart of
the Women's Liberation Movement.

00:13:52.433 --> 00:13:54.807
&gt;&gt; Well then I came here and

00:13:54.807 --> 00:14:00.520
Egon has introduced me to
Diana Vreeland blah blah blah.

00:14:00.520 --> 00:14:02.915
Anyway, so I yes, and

00:14:02.915 --> 00:14:08.215
then this dress, this, so for
two years I had two children in 13 months.

00:14:08.215 --> 00:14:13.155
So I had one child, and then I was
pregnant again, and in between back and

00:14:13.155 --> 00:14:19.380
forth to go to Italy and I had miserable
little orders 15 pieces 55 pieces.

00:14:19.380 --> 00:14:23.260
And the Italian men would say, " What are
you crazy, I don't have a sample room I

00:14:23.260 --> 00:14:28.160
have a factory." I said, " Please, please,
please trust me, have faith in me.

00:14:28.160 --> 00:14:32.020
So I would come back with more samples and
like that for two years.

00:14:32.020 --> 00:14:37.197
And I had a little wrap top and
the little wrap top was

00:14:37.197 --> 00:14:42.983
basically what dancers,
ballerinas wear to keep warm,

00:14:42.983 --> 00:14:49.393
in French it's called cache coeur and
it's like hides heart.

00:14:49.393 --> 00:14:53.676
By the way it's also
called HUb after fairy,

00:14:53.676 --> 00:14:58.878
which is wallet,
which it ended up filling my wallet.

00:14:58.878 --> 00:15:03.747
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH]
&gt;&gt; So at first it was a little

00:15:03.747 --> 00:15:09.260
top with a matching skirt and
that did really, really well.

00:15:09.260 --> 00:15:13.670
And then I said well we have to why
don't we make it into a dress, and

00:15:13.670 --> 00:15:14.660
that was a dress.

00:15:16.490 --> 00:15:21.618
And then I started I was
22 by the time I was 26 and

00:15:21.618 --> 00:15:26.755
seven we were making
25,000 dresses a week.

00:15:26.755 --> 00:15:31.597
&gt;&gt; Wow
&gt;&gt; And that's 50.000 sleeves,

00:15:31.597 --> 00:15:33.655
and that's a lot.

00:15:33.655 --> 00:15:34.719
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH]
&gt;&gt; And then I was

00:15:34.719 --> 00:15:36.870
living this American dream and.

00:15:36.870 --> 00:15:37.883
&gt;&gt; I read that a dress.

00:15:37.883 --> 00:15:44.607
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH]
[APPLAUSE]

00:15:44.607 --> 00:15:47.343
&gt;&gt; But what I'm trying to say is that

00:15:47.343 --> 00:15:54.350
people say I made the dress, yes I made
the dress but really the dress made me.

00:15:54.350 --> 00:16:01.813
Because thanks to this dress,
I became more confident.

00:16:01.813 --> 00:16:08.283
I was going around women, wrapping them,
and I was feeling confident,

00:16:08.283 --> 00:16:14.590
so I was sharing my confidence,
and I was selling my confidence.

00:16:14.590 --> 00:16:18.683
And then it was a time of liberation.

00:16:18.683 --> 00:16:23.981
I mean if you were young in the 70's,
it was a very nice time to be young,

00:16:23.981 --> 00:16:29.125
because it was somewhere between
the pill and AIDs, you figure it out.

00:16:29.125 --> 00:16:32.169
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH]
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH] And so,

00:16:32.169 --> 00:16:36.899
it was a very nice time to be young.

00:16:36.899 --> 00:16:42.936
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH]
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH] Okay,

00:16:42.936 --> 00:16:48.527
well, [LAUGH] So I read that you

00:16:48.527 --> 00:16:54.565
named your first dress Angela,

00:16:54.565 --> 00:17:00.801
after the [CROSSTALK]
&gt;&gt; Angela Davis, yeah, you know why?

00:17:00.801 --> 00:17:02.746
Funny, where you read that?

00:17:02.746 --> 00:17:05.222
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH]
&gt;&gt; No, there was-

00:17:05.222 --> 00:17:06.400
&gt;&gt; I do my research.

00:17:06.400 --> 00:17:09.827
&gt;&gt; There was a dress that had stripes.

00:17:09.827 --> 00:17:14.194
And, I mean it wasn't a very good
reason why I called in Angela Davis,

00:17:14.194 --> 00:17:17.687
because she had been arrested,
and it's prescribed.

00:17:17.687 --> 00:17:22.508
&gt;&gt; But what were you hoping I think
it was a really bold move to name

00:17:22.508 --> 00:17:25.590
your dress after the civil rights act.

00:17:25.590 --> 00:17:27.727
&gt;&gt; Yeah but nobody knew,

00:17:27.727 --> 00:17:32.652
I don't even know how you know [LAUGH]
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH]

00:17:32.652 --> 00:17:35.325
&gt;&gt; But by the way I loved Angela Davis,

00:17:35.325 --> 00:17:38.724
she was so beautiful,
I mean she was strong.

00:17:38.724 --> 00:17:43.370
Yes, there were all these women that
poped out that this time there was

00:17:43.370 --> 00:17:47.228
Angela Davis and Gloria Steinem,
and it was a wonderful,

00:17:47.228 --> 00:17:50.632
I mean actually, okay,
I was a princess, right?

00:17:50.632 --> 00:17:55.715
Because I had married a prince,
but actually at the time this

00:17:55.715 --> 00:18:00.509
one Gloria Steinem invented the word Ms,
M-S, right?

00:18:00.509 --> 00:18:04.312
So you were either Ms. or Mrs.

00:18:04.312 --> 00:18:08.846
you were Ms, and I actually traded my

00:18:08.846 --> 00:18:13.639
title of princess to be a Ms.
&gt;&gt; [APPLAUSE]

00:18:13.639 --> 00:18:14.635
&gt;&gt; Because I'm a feminist.

00:18:14.635 --> 00:18:16.150
&gt;&gt; Yes.

00:18:16.150 --> 00:18:16.964
&gt;&gt; Yes.

00:18:16.964 --> 00:18:22.620
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH]
&gt;&gt; Sorry.

00:18:22.620 --> 00:18:28.123
&gt;&gt; Well, its kind of in fashion to be
a feminist again, it went away for awhile.

00:18:28.123 --> 00:18:33.369
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH]
&gt;&gt; So there's a movement that's happening

00:18:33.369 --> 00:18:38.011
right now with women, and
a lot that has come up aground,

00:18:38.011 --> 00:18:42.700
the Me Too movement,
the Times Up movement.

00:18:42.700 --> 00:18:46.943
From your experience with
the Women's Separation Movement in the 70s

00:18:46.943 --> 00:18:50.707
to what's happening now,
what do you think are the parallels?

00:18:50.707 --> 00:18:52.790
Or what do you think are the differences?

00:18:54.210 --> 00:18:57.734
And how do you think that we're
fighting for in extension of something,

00:18:57.734 --> 00:18:59.829
the same thing, how do you think about it?

00:18:59.829 --> 00:19:02.778
&gt;&gt; So when I was young,

00:19:02.778 --> 00:19:08.331
we were feminists because we wanted to

00:19:08.331 --> 00:19:13.373
have equality, and in many ways.

00:19:13.373 --> 00:19:16.610
And so that was that.

00:19:16.610 --> 00:19:21.339
But at the time, I grew up that if you

00:19:21.339 --> 00:19:25.911
were a woman you had your period and

00:19:25.911 --> 00:19:31.282
you would get groped, that's just it.

00:19:31.282 --> 00:19:32.800
That would happen.

00:19:32.800 --> 00:19:37.539
Men, your bosses would treat
you somehow like meat, and

00:19:37.539 --> 00:19:42.092
you just had to defend yourself
the best way you could.

00:19:42.092 --> 00:19:47.445
But it was part of
the DNA of being a woman.

00:19:47.445 --> 00:19:52.716
So, and I was lucky because first
of all I only had three bosses,

00:19:52.716 --> 00:19:55.659
and they were all before I was 22.

00:19:55.659 --> 00:20:00.920
And also because then I had my own
business, and so I didn't have a boss.

00:20:00.920 --> 00:20:05.372
And then I also was in the fashion
industry where women were actually,

00:20:05.372 --> 00:20:07.722
it was easier for a woman to.

00:20:07.722 --> 00:20:14.226
But I'm thinking, now all these
other women who had jobs with

00:20:14.226 --> 00:20:20.120
male bosses who constantly
will touch them and do that.

00:20:20.120 --> 00:20:25.019
And the fact that they didn't want to lose
their jobs because they have children,

00:20:25.019 --> 00:20:27.437
because you don't want to lose your job.

00:20:27.437 --> 00:20:31.587
You like your job, you study for
your job, you have a good job, and

00:20:31.587 --> 00:20:33.606
then you have this pig as a boss.

00:20:33.606 --> 00:20:35.310
And how'd you deal with it?

00:20:35.310 --> 00:20:43.069
And so,
I think what happened recently is great.

00:20:43.069 --> 00:20:47.869
I think that it's one step ahead, and

00:20:47.869 --> 00:20:52.790
that man cannot treat us as meat.

00:20:52.790 --> 00:20:58.669
As we can't treat them as something else,
whatever it is.

00:20:58.669 --> 00:21:01.671
But it's a good thing,
it's a very good thing.

00:21:01.671 --> 00:21:06.926
And as women and
as mothers I think that it's

00:21:06.926 --> 00:21:11.614
our responsibility to raise our sons so

00:21:11.614 --> 00:21:14.750
that they respect women.

00:21:15.920 --> 00:21:22.990
But it's so much ingrained in society, but

00:21:22.990 --> 00:21:28.349
eventually it's I don't know.

00:21:28.349 --> 00:21:34.404
My mother always raised me like it
was an advantage to be a woman,

00:21:34.404 --> 00:21:37.670
so I felt that it was an advantage.

00:21:37.670 --> 00:21:42.353
But maybe I was very privileged,

00:21:42.353 --> 00:21:47.330
maybe I had good legs, who knows?

00:21:47.330 --> 00:21:52.698
But the truth is that I have never
met a woman who is not strong,

00:21:52.698 --> 00:21:54.668
all women are strong.

00:21:54.668 --> 00:22:00.242
But, a brother, a father, a religion,
or very often themselves,

00:22:00.242 --> 00:22:04.277
ourselves, we don't want
to show our strength,

00:22:04.277 --> 00:22:09.271
because we want our men to feel strong and
blah, blah, blah,

00:22:09.271 --> 00:22:13.230
and powerful and
all of that, which is fine.

00:22:13.230 --> 00:22:17.134
But then there's a tragedy, there's
a fire, there's something, and the woman

00:22:17.134 --> 00:22:21.284
gets one child, then the other child, and
the jewelry, and bam, boom, off the door.

00:22:21.284 --> 00:22:24.738
And you see that at the end
of the time of tragedy,

00:22:24.738 --> 00:22:28.710
the woman strength comes out and
she takes over.

00:22:28.710 --> 00:22:36.028
So, I think it's important for women to
know inside of them that they are strong.

00:22:36.028 --> 00:22:41.422
And it is their decision to show it or
not to show it.

00:22:41.422 --> 00:22:47.560
But what is the most important thing
is that they know it inside themselves.

00:22:47.560 --> 00:22:52.798
&gt;&gt; I think it's an interesting
concept to talk about the privilege

00:22:52.798 --> 00:22:57.454
that may be associated with
some women in their careers,

00:22:57.454 --> 00:23:02.980
as early as to not having to
deal with that of harassment.

00:23:02.980 --> 00:23:06.751
And I know you had three bosses
before you were 22, right?

00:23:06.751 --> 00:23:11.570
I think there are a lot of women in
a lot of different industries that don't

00:23:11.570 --> 00:23:13.240
have that privilege.

00:23:13.240 --> 00:23:13.873
&gt;&gt; That's right.

00:23:13.873 --> 00:23:17.779
&gt;&gt; So,
particularly in the fashion industry,

00:23:17.779 --> 00:23:21.786
when you have an industry
that the success is so

00:23:21.786 --> 00:23:25.607
contingent on young women, young models.

00:23:25.607 --> 00:23:30.676
For your role as the CDFA President, which
is the Council of the Fashion Designers

00:23:30.676 --> 00:23:34.803
of America, how do you think about
protecting these young women?

00:23:34.803 --> 00:23:39.934
&gt;&gt; What we do, we have rules, we have
all kinds of things that we set up,

00:23:39.934 --> 00:23:42.420
and it is incredibly important.

00:23:42.420 --> 00:23:48.804
Because I mean, I did try to be
a model completely not successful,

00:23:48.804 --> 00:23:52.687
but I mean that is all about rejections.

00:23:52.687 --> 00:23:57.620
I mean, it's just the worst thing
To try to want to be a model

00:23:57.620 --> 00:24:00.898
because it's the worst.

00:24:00.898 --> 00:24:06.139
But no, as the CMDA, we are making rules,

00:24:06.139 --> 00:24:11.210
and we're doing this more and more.

00:24:11.210 --> 00:24:16.842
But it's also-
&gt;&gt; And I think it's also interesting for

00:24:16.842 --> 00:24:19.807
the fashion industry in particular,

00:24:19.807 --> 00:24:23.156
because women are the prime consumers-
&gt;&gt; Right, right.

00:24:23.156 --> 00:24:24.510
&gt;&gt; Most of the consumers.

00:24:24.510 --> 00:24:29.040
And you do have a number of very
powerful women within that industry.

00:24:30.430 --> 00:24:34.834
But there still seems to be this power
dynamic where women are still subjected

00:24:34.834 --> 00:24:38.490
to a lot of the same things that
they are in other industries, and

00:24:38.490 --> 00:24:39.928
why do you think that is?

00:24:43.798 --> 00:24:45.085
&gt;&gt; Because we're stronger.

00:24:45.085 --> 00:24:51.090
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH]
&gt;&gt; Listen, women have to stick together,

00:24:51.090 --> 00:24:55.760
but the first thing is they have
to feel strong women themselves.

00:24:55.760 --> 00:25:01.910
And they just have to, we have to deal
with what we have to deal, but together

00:25:01.910 --> 00:25:07.150
we have to make sure that we support each
other and industries have to support.

00:25:07.150 --> 00:25:10.699
And I think that all the things,
the Me Too and

00:25:10.699 --> 00:25:14.256
Tme's up as really, really being helpful.

00:25:14.256 --> 00:25:16.350
And it is a different time, you know?

00:25:16.350 --> 00:25:21.375
And we have to make sure that,
that doesn't go away..

00:25:21.375 --> 00:25:27.281
&gt;&gt; I know you spend a lot of your time and
your resources, whether social or

00:25:27.281 --> 00:25:32.506
financial, on empowering women,
on women's empowerment.

00:25:32.506 --> 00:25:36.479
And that you created the DVF
awards not long ago.

00:25:36.479 --> 00:25:37.153
Can you tell us a bit about that?

00:25:37.153 --> 00:25:43.967
&gt;&gt; Well, next year is going to
be the 10th anniversary, yeah.

00:25:43.967 --> 00:25:49.056
Yes, it's actually my son who said,
you do so much for women,

00:25:49.056 --> 00:25:54.258
you should really reward them and
give them money and do a prize.

00:25:54.258 --> 00:25:56.815
And then one day it will
be like a Nobel Prize.

00:25:56.815 --> 00:26:02.110
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH]
&gt;&gt; And so we started together

00:26:02.110 --> 00:26:07.820
when I got involved in creating
the women's conference with Tina Brown.

00:26:07.820 --> 00:26:15.203
And then at that time
I started this prize.

00:26:15.203 --> 00:26:19.288
And it was really
thinking about my mother.

00:26:19.288 --> 00:26:22.530
My mother was a survivor of Auschwitz.

00:26:22.530 --> 00:26:27.270
She survived, and so it's for

00:26:27.270 --> 00:26:31.170
women who have the strength to fight,

00:26:31.170 --> 00:26:35.160
the courage to survive, and
the leadership to inspire.

00:26:35.160 --> 00:26:40.467
And the women we have given
awards to are just remarkable,

00:26:40.467 --> 00:26:47.197
most of them are not that well known but
they've done an incredible work.

00:26:47.197 --> 00:26:50.310
And it's been wonderful, wonderful.

00:26:50.310 --> 00:26:54.700
And then you create a family over ten
years, and then you see what they got and

00:26:54.700 --> 00:26:58.623
usually they got other prices after,
and then you get to know them.

00:26:58.623 --> 00:27:04.300
And I think that what is most important
is, I believe in the chain of love.

00:27:04.300 --> 00:27:09.230
If you pay attention to people and
you know them, and then you help them.

00:27:09.230 --> 00:27:11.567
I really do believe in that.

00:27:11.567 --> 00:27:18.199
I mean, I really believe, you see,
now I was very successful, very young.

00:27:18.199 --> 00:27:20.134
Blah, blah, blah, I created a company.

00:27:20.134 --> 00:27:24.694
Obviously when you create something
it went up, it went down,

00:27:24.694 --> 00:27:26.620
it had many generations.

00:27:26.620 --> 00:27:31.482
But now I have a woman CEO that I think
is going to really take the company

00:27:31.482 --> 00:27:34.563
to the new worldm, the new digital world.

00:27:34.563 --> 00:27:39.192
And so what I want to do for
the rest of my life now that I am

00:27:39.192 --> 00:27:42.917
70 years old is the fact
that I want to use,

00:27:42.917 --> 00:27:47.572
because when you are successful,
two things happen.

00:27:47.572 --> 00:27:52.000
One, you can pay your bills,
and two, you have a voice.

00:27:52.000 --> 00:27:57.246
And when you have a voice,
its important, it's your duty, but

00:27:57.246 --> 00:28:03.678
it's also a gift and a privilege to use
your voice for people who have no voice.

00:28:03.678 --> 00:28:07.934
And that voicing and
that being able to share

00:28:07.934 --> 00:28:12.881
the experience that I had,
because I may be 70 but

00:28:12.881 --> 00:28:16.725
I really should be 140,
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH]

00:28:16.725 --> 00:28:18.690
&gt;&gt; For how much I have lived and

00:28:18.690 --> 00:28:24.515
the different people I've met and the
different circumstances and all of that.

00:28:24.515 --> 00:28:28.532
To be able to share that, and
to share my experience, and

00:28:28.532 --> 00:28:31.480
my knowledge, and all of that.

00:28:31.480 --> 00:28:35.970
And so that's what I would like to do for
the rest of my life.

00:28:35.970 --> 00:28:40.110
And I know you all study business,
and you're all super smart,

00:28:40.110 --> 00:28:41.660
and everything else.

00:28:41.660 --> 00:28:46.511
And so what I would like to be able to
tell people is that, women mostly, and

00:28:46.511 --> 00:28:50.977
this is very much of a women's audience,
so the other day I spoke to,

00:28:50.977 --> 00:28:54.220
I walked in there,
they were all male engineers.

00:28:54.220 --> 00:28:56.632
&gt;&gt; I see a couple of men.

00:28:56.632 --> 00:28:58.594
&gt;&gt; I said, it's not going to go well mem,
but it did very well.

00:28:58.594 --> 00:29:04.680
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH]
&gt;&gt; Anyway, and is my big umbrella now.

00:29:04.680 --> 00:29:08.804
My big umbrella is design your life,
right?

00:29:08.804 --> 00:29:13.686
And so because I want to do podcast and
I want to do speaking engagements and

00:29:13.686 --> 00:29:18.510
all of that, I wanted this summer,
I said, okay, I have to structure.

00:29:18.510 --> 00:29:21.400
What does it mean to design in your life,
right?

00:29:21.400 --> 00:29:22.550
What does it mean?

00:29:22.550 --> 00:29:28.530
And so because I was in Sicily,
I drew a Greek temple,

00:29:28.530 --> 00:29:31.950
and on top of the roof I
wrote design your life.

00:29:31.950 --> 00:29:34.730
And then I put the pillars.

00:29:34.730 --> 00:29:38.250
And these are the pillars
that I came up with.

00:29:38.250 --> 00:29:46.220
The first pillar, the most important
pillar we all have is our character.

00:29:46.220 --> 00:29:50.402
Because you could lose everything,
you could lose your health,

00:29:50.402 --> 00:29:56.043
you could lose your wealth, you could lose
your children, you could lose everything.

00:29:56.043 --> 00:30:00.860
But you never lose your character,
even under torture.

00:30:00.860 --> 00:30:03.850
And what does character really mean?

00:30:03.850 --> 00:30:10.650
Character's really about
the relationship you have with yourself.

00:30:10.650 --> 00:30:15.450
And to have a relationship with
yourself is hard because it means you

00:30:15.450 --> 00:30:16.730
have to be demanding.

00:30:16.730 --> 00:30:19.410
You have to be disciplined.

00:30:19.410 --> 00:30:22.910
You have to do all of these things,

00:30:22.910 --> 00:30:27.872
but you have yourself as a refuge,
as a shelter.

00:30:27.872 --> 00:30:31.917
And no matter what happens,
you always have yourself.

00:30:31.917 --> 00:30:40.430
And if you have that, any other
relationship is a plus and not a must.

00:30:40.430 --> 00:30:46.160
And there is nothing less
attractive than being needy.

00:30:46.160 --> 00:30:50.450
So character is really
the pillar number one.

00:30:50.450 --> 00:30:54.917
And in order to, you practice it everyday,

00:30:54.917 --> 00:30:59.160
it's like plumbing, or pruning trees.

00:30:59.160 --> 00:31:01.530
It's all the time you need to work at it.

00:31:01.530 --> 00:31:05.960
You need to have time, a little bit
of solitude, a little bit of silence,

00:31:05.960 --> 00:31:10.990
a little bit of things so that you know
that somehow you could rely on yourself.

00:31:12.090 --> 00:31:15.220
Pillar number one.

00:31:15.220 --> 00:31:19.630
That's why I say the first,
number one lesson in life

00:31:19.630 --> 00:31:23.260
is the most important relationship in
life is the one you have with yourself.

00:31:25.180 --> 00:31:31.990
Pillar number two is your body and
your mind, your brain.

00:31:31.990 --> 00:31:35.440
Our body, we have so
much power on our body.

00:31:35.440 --> 00:31:37.090
We can exercise it.

00:31:37.090 --> 00:31:38.740
We can forget about it.

00:31:38.740 --> 00:31:41.640
We can modify the shape.

00:31:41.640 --> 00:31:46.270
And mind,
it's only recently that I realized,

00:31:46.270 --> 00:31:48.760
we have this incredible computer inside.

00:31:48.760 --> 00:31:52.366
I can speak three languages at
the same time, and drive and

00:31:52.366 --> 00:31:54.730
drink and all of that at the same time.

00:31:56.260 --> 00:31:59.950
And my mind is making me do all that.

00:31:59.950 --> 00:32:02.052
I mean, how does that work?

00:32:02.052 --> 00:32:02.565
&gt;&gt; It's amazing.

00:32:02.565 --> 00:32:07.920
&gt;&gt; I mean that is just amazing, but
we have to pay attention to it.

00:32:07.920 --> 00:32:11.950
And not take stupid drugs that will

00:32:11.950 --> 00:32:15.090
modify our brain, anyway-
&gt;&gt; You mean like taking

00:32:15.090 --> 00:32:16.750
care of it physically?

00:32:16.750 --> 00:32:20.460
&gt;&gt; Yes, so one, two, three, heart, heart.

00:32:21.970 --> 00:32:28.210
Heart is a muscle, right,
that pumps the blood and creates oxygen.

00:32:28.210 --> 00:32:31.150
So heart is life, life is love.

00:32:32.330 --> 00:32:40.160
Love is compassion, is emotion, and
it's about to be a human, a human being.

00:32:40.160 --> 00:32:46.090
I know everybody talks about artificial
intelligence and all of these things,

00:32:46.090 --> 00:32:52.740
but I really do think that the only way
we can save humanity is by being humans.

00:32:52.740 --> 00:32:57.390
And I think that there's going to be, I
hear it, I hear a lot of people who all of

00:32:57.390 --> 00:33:03.208
a sudden think about being kind and
doing things to be human.

00:33:03.208 --> 00:33:06.005
And I think-
&gt;&gt; Does that mean empathy to you?

00:33:06.005 --> 00:33:10.728
&gt;&gt; Yes, and it means paying
attention to the next person,

00:33:10.728 --> 00:33:13.375
and yeah, it's being human.

00:33:13.375 --> 00:33:16.770
All of sudden being human and
having a heart.

00:33:16.770 --> 00:33:19.540
I think that's the next trend, hopefully.

00:33:20.570 --> 00:33:24.730
I mean we have to compete against
whatever else is happening.

00:33:24.730 --> 00:33:29.970
So, one, two, three, four,
pillar number four is purpose.

00:33:29.970 --> 00:33:36.600
And purpose is work, is therefore work
is your independence, your identity,

00:33:36.600 --> 00:33:42.300
it's your expression,
it's who you are in society.

00:33:42.300 --> 00:33:46.673
So I know you worry a lot about that here,
so I shouldn't talk about it anymore.

00:33:46.673 --> 00:33:50.192
And one purpose and you have-
&gt;&gt; [INAUDIBLE]

00:33:50.192 --> 00:33:53.559
&gt;&gt; And then you have what I do which is

00:33:53.559 --> 00:33:59.454
aesthetics, what you wear,
how you surround yourself,

00:33:59.454 --> 00:34:02.837
the power of color, all of that.

00:34:02.837 --> 00:34:06.560
And that's what I thought
my five pillars were.

00:34:06.560 --> 00:34:11.733
And then two weeks ago I was talking
to my 17 year old grandson and

00:34:11.733 --> 00:34:14.098
he said, you forgot nature.

00:34:14.098 --> 00:34:17.120
And I thought, yeah,
that's right, I forgot nature.

00:34:17.120 --> 00:34:19.150
So we added another pillar.

00:34:19.150 --> 00:34:25.435
And because it was six, and I like odd
numbers, I had to add another, seven.

00:34:25.435 --> 00:34:31.620
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH]
&gt;&gt; And my pillar number seven was rituals.

00:34:32.660 --> 00:34:37.766
We do things every day that
we don't even think about.

00:34:37.766 --> 00:34:43.590
Many years ago I did three coffee table
books, they were photography books.

00:34:43.590 --> 00:34:47.470
And one was called The Bed,
the Bath, and the Table.

00:34:47.470 --> 00:34:52.020
And the bed, if you think about it,
people, we throw

00:34:52.020 --> 00:34:56.070
ourselves in the bed without thinking and
we go and sleep in a messy bed.

00:34:56.070 --> 00:35:00.740
But look at what the bed, I mean how
important, we're conceived in a bed,

00:35:00.740 --> 00:35:04.568
we make love in a bed,
we're sick in a bed, we die in a bed.

00:35:04.568 --> 00:35:10.150
I mean so the ritual of the bed and
the ritual of the table,

00:35:10.150 --> 00:35:14.130
what to eat and how to eat and
the pleasure of that.

00:35:14.130 --> 00:35:17.650
I think there's a whole
ritualist things that we do.

00:35:17.650 --> 00:35:22.780
Or even the bathroom,
the privacy of the bathroom and whatever.

00:35:22.780 --> 00:35:27.040
So all of this is the design of your life.

00:35:27.040 --> 00:35:30.079
And does that sound interesting to you?

00:35:30.079 --> 00:35:31.204
&gt;&gt; Yes.

00:35:31.204 --> 00:35:32.284
&gt;&gt; Why?
&gt;&gt; [APPLAUSE]

00:35:32.284 --> 00:35:32.904
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH]

00:35:32.904 --> 00:35:34.073
&gt;&gt; [APPLAUSE]

00:35:34.073 --> 00:35:35.220
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH]

00:35:35.220 --> 00:35:35.720
&gt;&gt; Why?

00:35:35.720 --> 00:35:37.374
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH]

00:35:37.374 --> 00:35:38.769
&gt;&gt; I mean I don't know,

00:35:38.769 --> 00:35:43.355
I mean I said it to somebody and
they said, well that's very generic.

00:35:43.355 --> 00:35:44.646
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH]

00:35:44.646 --> 00:35:47.738
&gt;&gt; I say, yeah, it's generic, but

00:35:47.738 --> 00:35:51.460
what isn't generic?
&gt;&gt; I think what isn't generic

00:35:51.460 --> 00:35:55.660
is the experience that your mother had,
and

00:35:55.660 --> 00:36:00.270
the influence that she's had on you as
a result of her surviving Auschwitz.

00:36:01.540 --> 00:36:05.876
You talked about that a lot in your book,
The Woman I Wanted To Be.

00:36:05.876 --> 00:36:10.286
And I would love to know
how that that influenced

00:36:10.286 --> 00:36:13.269
the woman that you wanted to be?

00:36:17.230 --> 00:36:21.761
&gt;&gt; All right, so
in order to describe my mother,

00:36:21.761 --> 00:36:29.100
imagine yourself in 1944, in Belgium,
occupied Belgium by German.

00:36:29.100 --> 00:36:34.231
This 22 year old girl gets arrested,

00:36:34.231 --> 00:36:40.776
deported, and
sent to labor camps in Auschwitz.

00:36:40.776 --> 00:36:46.270
It was, because it was so late, I mean she
stayed there for a while and then there

00:36:46.270 --> 00:36:52.120
was the famous death march and they went
to another camp and then another camp.

00:36:52.120 --> 00:36:57.060
And 13 months later she ended
up in this third camp, and

00:36:57.060 --> 00:36:59.310
by then she was in pretty bad shape.

00:36:59.310 --> 00:37:03.480
And one morning they woke up and

00:37:03.480 --> 00:37:08.540
the Germans had left, and then the Russian
came and raped them and then whatever.

00:37:08.540 --> 00:37:11.160
And then they left, and
then the Americans came.

00:37:11.160 --> 00:37:15.930
And she was in a very bad state,
she couldn't move.

00:37:15.930 --> 00:37:20.280
She was brought to a hospital,
she weighed 49 pounds.

00:37:20.280 --> 00:37:25.600
And then so
they made her fill a questionnaire.

00:37:25.600 --> 00:37:32.770
The only reason I know that is because the
Holocaust Museum who I help, that I help,

00:37:32.770 --> 00:37:37.538
did a huge research on both my parents and
sent me all this information.

00:37:37.538 --> 00:37:42.600
And this questionnaire comes in a way,
say name, first name, second name,

00:37:43.850 --> 00:37:48.880
where you came from, and
then state of health.

00:37:48.880 --> 00:37:52.408
And even though she couldn't have
moved she wrote excellent health.

00:37:52.408 --> 00:37:56.920
And that explains everything.

00:37:56.920 --> 00:37:58.970
No matter what she always told me,

00:37:58.970 --> 00:38:02.350
no matter what happens,
you can never be a victim.

00:38:03.390 --> 00:38:07.339
And she survived,
she gained the weight back,

00:38:07.339 --> 00:38:12.285
her fiance came back from Switzerland,
they got married.

00:38:12.285 --> 00:38:16.835
The doctor says, you have to wait three to
five years before you have a child because

00:38:16.835 --> 00:38:19.960
you won't make it and
the child won't be normal.

00:38:19.960 --> 00:38:22.652
And nine months later I was born and
I was not normal.

00:38:22.652 --> 00:38:29.360
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH]
[APPLAUSE]

00:38:29.360 --> 00:38:32.136
&gt;&gt; So my mother used to say to me,

00:38:32.136 --> 00:38:38.035
God saved me so that I could save,
so that I could give you life.

00:38:38.035 --> 00:38:43.540
By giving you life, you gave me my
life back, you are my total freedom.

00:38:43.540 --> 00:38:47.680
So I was raised with a lot of risk,

00:38:47.680 --> 00:38:52.937
I was supposed to be a total freedom, at
the age of one I had to speak in public,

00:38:52.937 --> 00:38:56.930
my birthday and I always had to memorize.

00:38:56.930 --> 00:38:59.170
My mother was a tiger mom.

00:38:59.170 --> 00:39:04.040
She was tough, and
she would not allow me to be afraid.

00:39:04.040 --> 00:39:08.220
If I was afraid of the dog,
she would lock me in the dog closet.

00:39:08.220 --> 00:39:09.695
Today she would be arrested.

00:39:09.695 --> 00:39:12.257
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH]
&gt;&gt; And, but

00:39:12.257 --> 00:39:17.381
after ten minutes you realize that
there's no reason to be afraid.

00:39:17.381 --> 00:39:25.390
And so obviously I'm very
much a result of my mother.

00:39:26.440 --> 00:39:30.200
So never be a victim, doesn't matter what
happened, blah, blah, blah, this and

00:39:30.200 --> 00:39:33.470
that, and that formed me.

00:39:33.470 --> 00:39:36.356
But when you have a very strong mother,

00:39:36.356 --> 00:39:40.833
it's you have to wait until she's gone for
you to accept it.

00:39:40.833 --> 00:39:43.270
&gt;&gt; So
we're going to do a quick lightning round.

00:39:43.270 --> 00:39:45.260
We have a couple minutes left.

00:39:45.260 --> 00:39:49.944
I would love for you to finish
this sentence, these sentences.

00:39:49.944 --> 00:39:57.215
I feel most confident when-
&gt;&gt; I feel most confident when,

00:40:00.350 --> 00:40:03.135
When I speak to a nice crowd.

00:40:03.135 --> 00:40:08.060
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH]
&gt;&gt; What angers me most is-

00:40:08.060 --> 00:40:10.590
&gt;&gt; I don't get angry very quickly.

00:40:10.590 --> 00:40:18.125
What angers me most is
when I witness lies.

00:40:18.125 --> 00:40:22.891
&gt;&gt; The thing people would be
surprised to learn about me is?

00:40:22.891 --> 00:40:27.876
&gt;&gt; The things that people would
be surprised to learn about me is

00:40:27.876 --> 00:40:34.873
that,
&gt;&gt; Probably-

00:40:34.873 --> 00:40:35.773
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH]

00:40:35.773 --> 00:40:37.165
&gt;&gt; You certainly do [LAUGH].

00:40:37.165 --> 00:40:42.640
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH]
&gt;&gt; My super power is?

00:40:44.126 --> 00:40:49.284
&gt;&gt; My super power is my
magic wand which I use

00:40:49.284 --> 00:40:55.957
every day when I try to,
my first e-mail of the day,

00:40:55.957 --> 00:41:02.803
I try tried to do something
that doesn't benefit me.

00:41:02.803 --> 00:41:08.718
And I will introduce one person to another
person, I don't have to leave a message,

00:41:08.718 --> 00:41:13.131
I don't have to speak,
all I have to do is write it perfectly and

00:41:13.131 --> 00:41:18.159
explain everything well, and
I may have changed that person's life.

00:41:20.174 --> 00:41:25.200
&gt;&gt; Most important advice I can give for
this audience is?

00:41:25.200 --> 00:41:30.840
&gt;&gt; Most important advice I can give is
the one I said before, is that the most

00:41:30.840 --> 00:41:36.128
important relationship you have is
the one you have with yourself.

00:41:36.128 --> 00:41:39.509
&gt;&gt; [APPLAUSE]

00:41:39.509 --> 00:41:44.319
[MUSIC]

